<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bridgnorth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bridgnorth is a market town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians</a>, built a burh, or fortified town, there in 912 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, who had camped nearby in 895–896. In 1102 Henry I besieged and took the castle of the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-belleme">Robert of Bellême</a> at Bridgnorth, and the town grew on its sandstone ridge above the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bridgnorth.jpg" alt="Bridgnorth" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gnesener1900 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bridgnorth%27s_High_Town.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bridgnorth is named after a bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a>, built further north than an earlier bridge at Quatford. The town lies in the Severn Valley and is divided into High Town on the west bank, standing on a promontory called Castle Hill, and Low Town on the east bank. High Town lies at 65 to 68 metres above sea level and Low Town at about 32 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest reference is in 895, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> made a camp at Cwatbridge. In 912 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd</a> built a mound on the west bank of the Severn, or possibly on the site of the later castle, as part of an offensive against the Danes. In 1101 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-belleme">Robert of Bellême, 3rd Earl of Shrewsbury</a>, moved from Quatford and built a castle and a church on the site of the present town, to defend against attacks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. The town became a royal borough on his attainder in 1102. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> walls of 1216 to 1223 were later rebuilt in stone, and the town was burnt by Roger Mortimer in 1322.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> freed the burgesses from toll throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1215. The burgesses sent two members to parliament from 1295 until 1867. In the Civil War Bridgnorth was one of the main Royalist strongholds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, and in 1646 Cromwell took it after a three-week siege and ordered the castle demolished. In 1808 the Hazledine ironworks in Low Town built Richard Trevithick's locomotive Catch Me Who Can.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bridgnorth is a market town and civil parish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, with 12,079 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The castle ruins lean at an angle of 15 degrees because of damage in the Civil War. Of the five town gates one survives, the Northgate, which houses the museum. The Cliff Railway, opened on 7 July 1892, links High Town and Low Town and is the only functional inland funicular railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Bishop Percy's House, built in 1580, survived the great fire of 1646 and was the birthplace of Thomas Percy, author of Reliques of Ancient English Poetry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bridgnorth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
